# 1. PLATFORM AND TECHNOLOGY REQUIREMENTS

Build a native Android app using:

- Kotlin.
- Jetpack Compose for the complete UI.
- Material 3 as the component foundation, with a custom Questly design system.
- Navigation Compose for navigation.
- ViewModel and StateFlow for UI state.
- Kotlin Coroutines for asynchronous operations.
- Room for persistent local storage.
- DataStore for user preferences.
- A repository pattern separating the UI, business logic, and storage.
- WorkManager for appropriate scheduled background work.
- Jetpack Glance for a future Android home-screen widget.
- Android notification APIs for optional reminders.
- Canvas or a suitable lightweight rendering approach for mini-games.
- Gradle Kotlin DSL for build configuration.

Use the versions compatible with the Android SDK and project template available in the development environment. Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies or incompatible libraries.

Organize the code into maintainable packages or modules such as:

- app
- core/designsystem
- core/database
- core/model
- core/domain
- feature/onboarding
- feature/home
- feature/quests
- feature/focus
- feature/analytics
- feature/arcade
- feature/achievements
- feature/profile
- feature/settings

If a multi-module setup is too heavy for the initial build, use a well-organized single application module with clear feature packages.

The app must compile. Do not leave missing imports, unresolved symbols, placeholder implementations, or nonfunctional buttons.

If the environment supports project inspection, inspect the existing files before editing. Preserve useful code and avoid generating duplicate projects.

